      "publicIdentifier": "haardikkk",
      "headline": "Blockchain Developer | Helping onboard developers to Web3",
      "about": "Did you know I don't have a first name? Yeah. Ask me about it.\n\nI was an annoyed 10 year old who couldn't deal with the slow process of earning coins in Club Penguin to buy accessories for your character. So I hacked it.\n\nI started developing with Visual Basic 6 at the time, and then moved on to a language called PAWN to make game servers for a popular online multiplayer mod called SA:MP. Wanting to expand my skills, I moved on to C# for 2-3 years, doing native Windows development.\n\nIt was a while after that NodeJS started to become pretty famous for revolutionizing Javascript. While playing around with NodeJS I came across an online chatroom called CoinChat (sadly, it's no longer available). The idea was simple, you engage in quality conversation there and you get rewarded with milli-BTC (Bitcoin was about $400 at the time) which you could spend back there for emojis and text colors. I developed a few text based multiplayer games which utilized user's mBTC for the platform. It was only a few weeks after that CoinChat got DDoS'ed and the community slowly got disrupt. This was my first introduction to Bitcoin.\n\nI got over it and moved on to developing trading software for virtual assets in games such as Team Fortress 2 and CS:GO. I developed hundreds of different bots over a couple of years. In early 2017, I got the chance to attend the European Summer Program on Rationality where I was reminded about Bitcoin again. I developed trading bots which swapped these virtual assets for Bitcoin instantly. Over the next year, I developed a huge interest in the field and learnt more about all the various blockchain platforms. \n\nThese days I am working on FlexFinTx, a self-sovereign identity platform for Sub-Saharan Africa built on the Algorand blockchain.",
